1 NAME Thomas /Place/ Sr.
2 GIVN Thomas
2 NSFX Sr.
2 _MARNM Place
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 APR 1734
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kingstown
3 STAE Rhode Island
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 JUN 1814
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint George
3 STAE Vermont
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 6 JUN 1814
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas Place, Sr. was born in April of 1734 in North Kingstown, Washington, Rhode Island Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas passed away in January of 1814 in St George, Chittenden, Vermont.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Place borrowed the sum of 500 pounds in 1796 from Job Place, (his cousin, whose father was Benajah Place of Foster). Another signer was Sollamon Place. Thomas Place described himself as a yeoman farmer in 1796. He could not
2 CONC  write. In 1790 he was a selectman of the Town of Hinesburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Hinesburg had only two settlers previous to the Revolution. In 1785, Thomas Place was one of 9 people who settled there.
2 CONT
2 CONT From notes regarding the village of Hinesburg: "In the winter of 1787 Mr. Thomas Place sent two sons (believed to be Roswell and Joseph) to fodder his cattle upon some hay near a beaver meadow. The day time was spent by the boys i
2 CONC n trapping sable, and the night in a cabin near the meadow. One night, while they were asleep, the cabin took fire. One of the young men awoke with his clothes in a blaze. He rushed out and by throwing himself down and rolling upo
2 CONC n the snow, he succeeded in quenching the flames. He then returned to rescue his brother (Joseph), but his utmost exertions were fruitless. The brother perished in the fire, and he was terribly burned in his efforts to save him. T
2 CONC hen in attempting to make his way to the nearest house--two miles away--through the woods and snow, he was severely frozen and only escaped death after a long confinement, with the almost total loss of his hands and a fire-scared 
2 CONC face."
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was in Scituate (later, Foster) at the time of the 1774 census, which indicates a family of 6 sons and 2 daughters. He would have been 42 at that time. He was also enumerated in Scituate, RI, in the 1777 Military Census of 
2 CONC Rhode Island. On 2 Oct 1779, Thomas and his wife, Bathsheba, sold 39 acres in Foster to his brother Benajah. On the 4th, they sold another 37 acres to his brother, Enoch Jr., and on the 5th, 14 acres to his brother Rufus. They sol
2 CONC d 80 acres on Cucumber Hill to Jedediah Pratt in 1779 as well. The settled in Bennington, VT., where daughter Nancy was married, and later in Clarendon, VT., where his daughter, Sarah was married in 1784, moving on to Hinesburg in
2 CONC  1785. On 22 Oct 1791, Thomas sold land to Job Place "of Foster, RI". The deed was witnessed by "Benager" Place and "Sollomon" Place. On 24 May 1797, Thomas Place of St. George, VT., deeded property to Job Place of Hinesburg, Geor
2 CONC ge and Augustus Place were witnesses. It appears that Thomas never moved--that the St. George-Hinesburg town line was drawn through his property and he ended up on the St. George side of the line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Birth record - Ancestry.com. Rhode Island, U.S., Vital Extracts, 1636-1899 [database on-line]. Lehi, UT, USA: Ancestry.com Operations, Inc., 2014. This collection was indexed by Ancestry World Archives Project contributors. Volu
2 CONC me Number and Title: Vol· 05: Washington County: Births, Marriages, Deaths. Ancestry Sharing Link
2 CONT ↑ Martson Watson. "Royal Families Americans of Royal and Noble Ancestry Volume Two Reverend Francis Marbury and Five Generations of His Descendants Through Anne (Marbury) Hutchinson and Katherine (Marbury) Scott)" Baltimore MD: Ge
2 CONC nealogical Publishing Co., Inc.; 2004
2 CONT ↑ Abby Maria Hemenway, compiler, "Vermont Historical Gazetteer", Vol. I, Part 2. Page 794 and 799.
2 CONT
