1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Personal Profile'''
2 CONT Birth: Born on May 27, 1710, in Woodstock/Pomfret, Windham County, CT.
2 CONT Parentage: He was the son of David Bishop (c. 1679–1717) and Rebecca Hubbard. His father, David Sr., was the son of Edward Bishop Jr., a notable figure in the Salem area.
2 CONT Death: He died in 1759 in Cheshire, New Hampshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sibling Connections'''
2 CONT David was part of a large family and had several siblings, most notably his brother John Bishop (born c. 1704), who you mentioned. After their father’s death in 1717, the family underwent legal changes: 
2 CONT Guardianship: In 1725, Samuel Bishop of Attleborough was appointed the legal guardian of David and his siblings—Rachel, Ebenezer, and John—as they were all above the age of 14.
2 CONT Brother John Bishop: John was born around 1704 and, like David, was a beneficiary of their father's estate and subject to the 1725 guardianship.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Settlement and Property:''' David settled in Chester after the 1725 guardianship proceedings in Massachusetts. At the time, Chester was part of the "Chestnut Country," and the first settlers were primarily granted lots in the "
2 CONC Old Village" or near Chester Street.
2 CONT '''The Bishop Farm:''' Like most men in Chester during the mid-1700s, David was primarily a farmer. Farms in this period were often roughly 50 to 100 acres, utilized for subsistence crops and livestock.
2 CONT Social Context: He was part of a close-knit group of families (including many from the Salem and Salisbury areas) who migrated to New Hampshire for cheaper land and religious freedom. His brother John Bishop (b. 1704) and other re
2 CONC latives are often listed in the same town histories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: While a specific headstone may no longer exist, the primary burial ground for residents of Chester at that time was the Old Settlers Cemetery (also known as the Village Cemetery), located near the center of the original to
2 CONC wn.Legacy: His death in 1759 preceded the massive expansion of the town. His children continued to appear in Chester and Rockingham County tax lists for decades following his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT While David died in Chester in 1759, his children and grandchildren often moved as new land opened up. Common migration patterns for Bishop descendants from Chester included moves to: 
2 CONT Pembroke or Concord, NH: Many Chester families moved slightly north toward the Merrimack Valley.
2 CONT Monmouth County, NJ or New York: Some branches of the Bishop family moved significantly further south or west after the French and Indian War.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''David Bishop''' (1710–1759) spent his final years in Chester, Rockingham County, New Hampshire, after migrating from Connecticut. His life there was defined by the transition of his family from the Massachusetts and Connecticut
2 CONC  colonies to the emerging settlements of New Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The First Meeting House (1731–1774)''' 
2 CONT This is the building David Bishop would have attended for religious services and town meetings. 
2 CONT Location: It stood on Chester Hill (the modern-day town center), roughly at the site where the Civil War Memorial is located today.
2 CONT Structure: Completed in 1732, it was a two-story wooden building measuring approximately 50 feet by 38 feet.
2 CONT Fate: It served the town until 1774, when it was outgrown and subsequently taken down after the completion of a newer building.The Village Cemetery, his probable burial place, was purchased by the town in 1751 and is located direc
2 CONC tly across from or very near this meeting house site. In colonial New England, the "meeting house" and the "burying ground" were almost always the physical and social anchor of the town center.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Father: David & Mother Rebekah:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT "Connecticut Births and Christenings, 1649-1906," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:F741-HTL : 11 February 2018), David Bishop, 27 Mar 1710; citing ; FHL microfilm unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both a wife and daughter named Anna. "Massachusetts Births and Christenings, 1639-1915," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VQDH-M9S : 10 February 2018), David Bishop in entry for Anna Bishop, 07 Oct 1
2 CONC 737; citing STURBRIDGE,WORCESTER,MASSACHUSETTS, ; FHL microfilm 1496523 IT 7.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Religious Affiliations'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On Oct. 1, 1756, David Bishop and a son David Bishop Jr. (III) and other members of the Bishop family are living in Woodstock.David is recorded as being born in Promfret, Vermont so the family must have spent some time there befor
2 CONC e removing to Woodstock. Woodstock was located on the old highway to Pomfret.
2 CONT
2 CONT They are involved in a disagreement disputing the church covenant of a Rev. Mr. Stiles who set up a church in Woodstock which resulted in them signing a petition to the Massachusetts legislature. This continued into 1757 with some
2 CONC  of their kinfolk signing a petition for both parties to settle their differences. 
2 CONT The Saybrook Platform was a new constitution for the Congregational church in Connecticut in 1708. Religious and civic leaders in Connecticut around 1700 were distressed by the colony-wide decline in personal religious piety and i
2 CONC n church discipline.  The colonial legislature took action by calling 12 ministers and four laymen to meet in Saybrook, Connecticut; eight were Yale trustees. They prepared fifteen articles that theologically put the church in the
2 CONC  Westminster theological tradition. It rejected extreme localism or "congregationalism" that had been inherited from England, replacing it with a centralized system similar to what the Presbyterians had.  The Congregational church
2 CONC  was now to be led by local ministerial associations and consociations composed of ministers and lay leaders from a specific geographical area. A colony-wide General Assembly had final authority.  Instead of the congregation from 
2 CONC each local church selecting its minister, the associations now had the responsibility to examine candidates for the ministry, and to oversee a behavior of the ministers.  The consociations (where laymen were powerless) could impos
2 CONC e discipline on specific churches and judge disputes that arose.  
2 CONT The result was a centralization of power that bothered many local church activists.  However the official associations responded by disfellowshipping churches that refused to comply.  The system worked for 150 years, guaranteeing 
2 CONC orthodox Puritanism. The Platform was conservative victory against a non-conformist tide which had begun with the Halfway Covenant and would culminate in the Great Awakening.[1][2]  Similar proposals for more centralized clerical 
2 CONC control of local churches were defeated in Massachusetts, where a much more liberal theology flourished.[3]  The Platform facilitated close ties with the Presbyterians; Connecticut Yankees who moved West founded Presbyterian churc
2 CONC hes.  [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/viewer/543938/?offset=0#page=653&viewer=picture&o=search&n=0&q=Bishop
2 CONT
2 CONT "Connecticut Births and Christenings, 1649-1906," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:F741-HTL : 11 February 2018), David Bishop, 27 Mar 1710; citing ; FHL microfilm unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both a wife and daughter named Anna. "Massachusetts Births and Christenings, 1639-1915," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VQDH-M9S : 10 February 2018), David Bishop in entry for Anna Bishop, 07 Oct 1
2 CONC 737; citing STURBRIDGE,WORCESTER,MASSACHUSETTS, ; FHL microfilm 1496523 IT 7.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Last child Marsilva Bishop born 1768, therefore he could not have died before 1767.'''
