2 CONT
2 CONT According to Chesterfield Town records, Nathan Bishop died in the Army in
2 CONT  1777.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Owing to the imperfect manner in which the army records were  kept during the struggle for independence, the names of the Ches  terfield men who were wounded or lost their lives, from any cause,  while in the service of their cou
2 CONC ntry, have not been ascertained,  except a few that have been obtained from other sources. Accord-  ing to the town-records, '''Nathan Bishop died in the army in 1 777-'''  It is said that
2 CONT = David Stoddard, Sen.=
2 CONT , went into the army, and never 
2 CONT returned. '''Elisha Bingham was discharged from the service, and  died while on his way home.''' Others there doubtless were who  lost their lives in the cause of liberty, and whose deeds, though  they themselves slumber in namele
2 CONC ss graves, will be held in remem-  brance by a grateful posterity for centuries to come."
2 CONT
2 CONT History Organized June 1775 at Fitzwilliam from Cheshire, Hillsborough and Cumberland Counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Entered Valley Forge with 369 men assigned, 237 fit for duty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Left Valley Forge with 316 assigned, 109 fit for duty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Previous engagements: Siege of Boston, Defense of Canada, Lake Champlain, Trenton-Princeton, Philadelphia-Monmouth
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Timeline of action:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Col.  James Reed:
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 June 1775, at the Battle of Bunker Hill
2 CONT
2 CONT  1 Jan, 1776, the 3rd Regiment was redesignated as the 2d Continental Regiment and assigned to Sullivan's Brigade
2 CONT
2 CONT 27 April 1776, the regiment was assigned to the Canadian Department[10] and was sent to Canada via New York City and then Albany via the Hudson River under Major General John Sullivan during the Invasion of Canada
2 CONT
2 CONT As the regiment arrived in Canada they suffered through a smallpox epidemic. They made it as far as Montreal where they were from June 12–17 and are shown there on Major Alexander Scammell's Return of the Continental Forces in Can
2 CONC ada
2 CONT
2 CONT The return of Reeds Regiment, dated 21 April 1776, showed 518 officers, staff, and rank and file, and then on General Horatio Gates' return, dated 24 August 1775, there were only 409 men in the regiment, an unfortunately typical l
2 CONC oss for regiments in the Canadian Department. The 3rd Regiment returned to Fort Ticonderoga on 16 July 1776, and were there until November 1776.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 3rd Regiment spent the late summer and fall of 1776 at Fort Ticonderoga. Reed's health was failing, and in November the state sent representatives who with the help of John Stark secured the re-enlistment of many New Hampshire
2 CONC  officers and soldiers and re-formed the 1st and 2nd New Hampshire Regiments with those men, which included many from the 3rd Regiment.[11] It is because of this that the 3rd Regiment is usually given credit for participating in t
2 CONC he Battle of Trenton and the Battle of Princeton, though in reality these men had re-enlisted or had been reorganized into these new 1st and 2nd New Hampshire Regiments. The 1st New Hampshire Regiment was also known as the 5th Reg
2 CONC iment of Foot and was commanded by John Stark, and the 2nd New Hampshire Regiment was also known as the 8th Regiment of Foot and was commanded by Enoch Poor. They left Fort Ticonderoga to join George Washington on November 16, 177
2 CONC 6
2 CONT
2 CONT About Poor's Brigade:
2 CONT
2 CONT Poor was born and raised in Andover, Massachusetts. His father, Thomas Poor had been part of the 1745 expedition that captured Louisburg, Nova Scotia, during King George's War. In 1755 young Poor enlisted as a private in one of th
2 CONC e Massachusetts units raised to accompany Jeffery Amherst's expedition to retake it during the French and Indian War. His unit enforced the expulsion of the Acadians. After the war, he came home to Andover, but only briefly. Poor 
2 CONC eloped with Martha Osgood, and the newlyweds settled in Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poor supported the separatists as early as the Stamp Act protests in 1765. He served on various committees for the town throughout the period of rising rebellion. In 1775 he was twice elected to the provincial Assembly. When the B
2 CONC attle of Lexington caused the assembly to call for three regiments of militia, Poor became the colonel of the 2nd New Hampshire Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT While the other regiments under colonels John Stark and James Reed were sent to Boston, the 2nd was stationed at Portsmouth and Exeter. After the Battle of Bunker Hill, they were also sent to Boston, arriving on June 25. In the su
2 CONC mmer of 1775, the unit was absorbed into the Continental Army. They were soon ordered into the Northern Department and went with General Richard Montgomery's invasion of Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the disaster in Canada, Poor led the survivors of his regiment in early 1776 back to Fort Ticonderoga. After refitting and recruiting, the unit was renamed as the 8th Continental regiment and joined Washington's main army in
2 CONC  December 1776 at winter quarters near Morristown, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1776, what remained of the 3rd Regiment was not much more than a corps of invalids and men who had not re-enlisted and whose enlistments would expire by the end of the year—about 250 in all. Those who did re-enlist in 
2 CONC November were incorporated into the 1st and 2nd Regiments. The main army at Fort Ticonderoga marched south to join Washington on 16 and 17 November 1776; the 3rd left Fort Ticonderoga two days later[13] for Fort George, at the sou
2 CONC thern end of Lake George, where they were assigned until relieved by a New Hampshire militia call-up at the end of December when the majority of the men's enlistments ran out. In December 1776, Col. Alexander Scammell was commissi
2 CONC oned to command the 3rd Regiment.[14] on 11 December 1776.[15] Some of the company commanders had been commissioned as of November 8, 1776, and were already in New Hampshire recruiting, and several more captains were added by Marc
2 CONC h 1777 to complete the regiment and recruiting. The result was a new 3rd Regiment for 1777 with an experienced officer corps, but with newly recruited rank and file soldiers, some of whom had had experience in the Continental Army
2 CONC  during the Siege of Boston in 1775.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 15 June 1777, the 3rd Regiment under Colonel Alexander Scammell was completed and present for duty at Fort Ticonderoga. The companies of the 3rd Regiment were commanded as follows 1st Company: Captain James Gray
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd Company: Captain Zachariah Beal
2 CONT
2 CONT 3rd Company: Captain Michael Mcclary
2 CONT
2 CONT 4th Company: Captain Daniel Livermore
2 CONT
2 CONT 5th Company: Captain Richard Weare
2 CONT
2 CONT 6th Company: Captain Benjamin Stone
2 CONT
2 CONT 7th Company: Captain Isaac Frye
2 CONT
2 CONT 8th Company: Captain William Ellis
2 CONT
2 CONT The 3rd Regiment did not stay long at Fort Ticonderoga; the fort's commander, Arthur St. Clair, was forced to abandon the fort because British commander John Burgoyne's forces had taken control of nearby Mount Defiance, making it 
2 CONC possible to easily cannonade Fort Ticonderoga. Thus, late on July 5, 1777, orders to leave Fort Ticonderoga were given, and by early morning on 6 July 1777 the 3rd Regiment was marching toward Hubbardton with the main portion of t
2 CONC he American army under St. Clair's command. By 8 July 1777 they had reached Fort Edward, New York, and began a campaign of damming streams and felling trees to make the countryside as impassable as possible for the invading Britis
2 CONC h Army. On 28 July 1777, the American Army moved to Fort Miller, then on 10 August 1777 to Stillwater, and on 18 August 1777 to Van Schaick Island at the confluence of the Mohawk and Hudson rivers. The American Army, now swelled b
2 CONC y post-harvest militia, then began moving north, arriving at Bemis Heights to camp on 8 September 1777. Most of the summer was spent without proper camp and supplies such as tents, given the hasty retreat from Fort Ticonderoga
2 CONT
2 CONT The monument to Gen. Poor, just a few feet from his burial site in Hackensack. The Congress named Poor a brigadier general on February 21, 1777. That spring, his brigade of three New Hampshire (1st, 2nd and 3rd) and two New York r
2 CONC egiments (2nd and 4th)was sent back to Ticonderoga. He withdrew with the rest of Arthur St. Clair's force of July 5. Moving south, they joined General Horatio Gates before the Battle of Saratoga, and his brigade was expanded by tw
2 CONC o regiments of Connecticut militia (Cook's and Latimer's).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first engagement of Saratoga, the Battle of Freeman's Farm, Poor's brigade was the first to come to the aid of Daniel Morgan's attack. Poor held the American left flank, extending into the woods and even wrapping around the
2 CONC  British position. They performed well, keeping General Simon Fraser's regulars engaged while Benedict Arnold led attacks on the central column.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the second engagement, the Battle of Bemis Heights, Poor's brigade was in General Benjamin Lincoln's division on the left (western) end of the American line. They were closest to the center of the advancing British, so they cam
2 CONC e under fire from the grenadier battalion of the British center. The fire was ineffective, so Major John Dyke Acland led the grenadiers in a bayonet charge. Poor held fire until they came very close, then opened up with the massed
2 CONC  fire of his 1,400 men. These were the first American shots in the battle. The charge was completely broken, and Acland himself fell wounded. With this collapse of Burgoyne's center, the Americans captured the wounded Acland and M
2 CONC ajor Williams along with the column's artillery. Poor then turned to his left and gave support to Ebenezer Learned and Morgan's men.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poor's brigade again spent the winter with the main army, this time at Valley Forge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War Record
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan Bishop (not Nathaniel Bishop who was also an American revolutionary war veteran)  is found in Chesterfield, Cheshire County, New Hampshire having sworn his " declaration" to fight the British on June 12, 1776 ( called " The
2 CONC  Association Test") alongside John Bishop. John was in Col. Nahum Baldwin/ Cpt. John Houghton's regiment in 1776. This regiment was sent to reinforce the Continental Army in the State of New York. Their discharge date has not been
2 CONC  ascertained.
2 CONT
2 CONT The men of Bennington in 1777 first fought at Ticonderoga marching there under Waitstill Scott in May 1777;  but immediately after returning, the New Hampshire milita men were called up again to march to the rescue  to fight again
2 CONC st General Burgoyne's advancement. And so under Lt. Col. Ashley,  Lt. Oliver Cobleigh marched with the men, nearly all of who were from Chesterfield, N.H. Another company in Ashley's regiment were commanded under James Robertson o
2 CONC f Chesterfield. They set out for Ticonderoga on June 29, 1777.  Another company of men marched from Chesterfield on July 22, 1777 which was the eighth company under Col. Moses Nichols, and Capt. Kimball Carlton to fight a Benningt
2 CONC on under General John Stark. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Background:
2 CONT
2 CONT The retreat of the American Continental Army from Fort Ticonderoga and the advance of the British Army was causing considerable alarm in Vermont and New Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Distrusting the aristocratic New Yorker, General Schuyler, who with General St Clair was suspected of treachery in giving up Fort Ticonderoga, the New Hampshire Council formed a militia brigade commanded by Brigadier John Stark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Six regiments appear to have been mustered from Chesterfield in 1776: Col. Timothy Bedal under Major Butterfield, who fought in Lower Canada, at " The Cedars".  Col. Nahum Baldwin ( regiment raised Sept. 1776 at Chesterfield). Col
2 CONC . Joshua Wingate and Cpt. William Humphrey ( reg't. Commanders, raised Chesterfield  July- August 1776 to reinforce the army in northern New York).  Col. Alexander Scammel's regiment ( Nov. 1776). Col. David Gilman's ( Dec. 1776).
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.nh.searchroots.com/documents/History_Chesterfield_NH.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pioneer Bishop family: http://jefferson.nygenweb.net/bishopp.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT LDS records state Nathan died "in the Army."
