2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Enoch Place, Sr.''' was born on 10 May 1702 in Kingston, Washington, Rhode Island, USA. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-22050312 Thomas Place, Jr.] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-22050318 Hannah (Cole) Place].
2 CONC  He died between 20 Dec 1787, date will written, and 26 Jan 1789, date will proved, in Foster, Providence, Rhode Island, United States. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Enoch married [https://www.geni.com/profile-57481846 Hannah (Wilcox) Place] on 31 in North Kingstown, Washington Co., RI. Together they had the following children born at North Kingstown, Washington, Rhode Island: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Thomas Place, b. 24 Apr 1732,  married Bathsheba Tyler 
2 CONT # Stephen Place, b. 21 Mar 1736,  married Martha Perkins 
2 CONT # Mary Place, b. 12 Jul 1739, married James Tyler
2 CONT # Benajah Place, b. Dec 28 1740, married Mary Perkins 
2 CONT # Martha Place, b. 1742, married Joshua King 
2 CONT # Peter Place, b. 25 Jun 1746
2 CONT # Rufus Place, b. 1747/48, married Abigail Perkins
2 CONT # Enoch Place, Jr., b. 1748, married Ruth Hammond 
2 CONT # George Place, b. 28 June 1749
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT He apparently had nine children, of whom six are named in the North Kingstown records with the birth years missing. Therefore, the birth order and dates may not be accurate and may have to rely on family histories. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From the "History of the John H. Place Family Homestead and Adjoining Farms with other interesting facts", by John W. Place, May, 1944" and "The Place Ancestry of John Warren Place, 3rd. By John W. Place, October 1946 Enoch and hi
2 CONC s wife Hannah Wilcox lived in North Kingston somewhere between The Hummocks and the main road, and moved to Moosup Valley, RI in 1751, where they bought 300 acres of land and lived in a house that stood where Leon Dexter lives now
2 CONC ." (1946)
2 CONT
2 CONT His fire-damaged birth record in North Kingstown says "(---)h Place was born the 10th day of May 170(---)." An article in The American Genealogist posits this was Enoch's birth and the year was 1702 based on circumstantial evidenc
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT "PLACE, Enoch, of Foster, yeoman. Will dated 20 Dec 1787, proved 26 Jan 1789, pgs 62-64. Mentions: Wife Hannah Place. Sons Thomas Place, Stephen Place, Benajah Place, & Enoch Place. Daughters Martha King & Mary Tyler dec wife of J
2 CONC ames Tyler. 6 Grandchildren children of James & my dec daughter Mary his wife, viz: Mary, George, Job, Esek, Ester, & John."
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenneth W Faig, Jr published a 'Phillips' Genealogy (The Moshassuck Press, 1993). 'The Place Relationships' to the Phillips family was included on pages 260-288.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Place Family Research Aid' (by Shirley Bucknum, Portland OR. 1983) The Enoch Place line through the marriage of Mary Place to James Tyler is discussed.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: In April, 1728, the General Assembly for the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantation admitted Enoch Place of North Kingston as a freeman. The records of the North Kingstown Town Council Meeting of Sept. 11, 1749, sh
2 CONC ow that "Enoch Place & wf and 6 chn, viz: Thos., Stephen, Benajah, George, Mary and Peter desired a certificate to E.G." They were certified to E. Greenwich on Sept. 17, 1749. (Violet E. Kettelle) On 16 May 1751 dated in Newport, 
2 CONC Stephen Herington (Harrington) of North Kingstown bought of Jonathan Thurston of Newport, merchant, for L1000 "One certain farm or tract of land in Scituate at a place called Westquanage Purchase (this Indian name is spelled vario
2 CONC usly, most commonly, Westcenaug) - 300 acres bounded north on land of Capt. Daniel Abbott, west partly by John Tyler and Stephen Foster, southerly by Caleb Vaughn and the line that divides the town of Scituate from Coventry (the C
2 CONC oventry line), easterly partly by Richard Waterman and Daniel Abbott, with all buildings, etc. Witnesses Josiah & Mary Lyndon. Scituate Bk IV p. 56. Stephen apparently had bought this for an investment for on 27 May 1751, "Stephen
2 CONC  Herington of North Kingstown in Kingstown, RI" sold this same piece to Enoch Place for L1500. His wife Elizabeth signed off her dower rights. Witnesses: John & Ann Gardiner. Scituate Bk IV p. 57. Enoch's purchase was in the weste
2 CONC rn part of Scituate which became Foster in 1781. The community that developed in the valley of the Moosup River, named for an Indian chief usually called Pessacus, became known as Moosup Valley in the second half of the 19th centu
2 CONC ry. (Margery Matthews)
2 CONT
2 CONT Enoch is shown in the town of Scituate at the time of the Military Census of Rhode Island of 1777, age 60+. Thomas, Stephen, Benijar, Enoch, "Rows" (Rufus?), and Joseph were also listed in Scituate between the ages of 16 and 50 an
2 CONC d "able to bear arms." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rhode Island Census of 1774 listed Thomas, Enoch, Stephen, and Benajah. These four families totalled 32 persons--8 males over 16, 11 males under 16, 8 females over 16 and 5 females under 16. The census of 1782 showed the famil
2 CONC ies in the town of Foster. Heads of households listed there include Benajah, Enoch Jr., Nathan, Rufus, Stephen, Stephen Jr., and Thomas. By this time, these 7 households totalled 48 persons including 14 boys and 11 girls under the
2 CONC  age of 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Enoch's will, written in 1787 and proven 26 Jan 1789, named 
2 CONT * wife Hannah, 
2 CONT * children Thomas, Stephen, Benajah, Rufus, Enoch Jr., and Martha King, 
2 CONT * and "the six children of James Tyler and daughter Mary, deceased." 
2 CONT * Land which Rufus inherited was described as "bounding on land Enoch sold to his brother Thomas." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Violet Kettelle notes: "The Town of Foster was set off from Scituate and founded in 1781. Thefollowing towns were all once part of Providence and were set off as indicated: Scituate, 1731; Glocester, 1731; Smithfield, 1731; Cranst
2 CONC on, 1754; Johnston, 1759; North Providence, 1765. Many people born before those dates in Providence who later appeared in one of these towns probably hadn't moved. The names of their towns had simply changed."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Enoch Place In Names & Stories in Newspapers from OldNews.com (Massachusetts, Maine, New Hampshire, Vermont) < [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-12013-1041084174-/enoch-place-in-names-stories-in-newspapers-from-oldnewsco
2 CONC m-massachusetts-maine-new-hampshire?s=OYYV7ZF3UROXMO4VNAUMX3BCTCTVIGI&indId=externalindividual-714ab90242fa8c6d2083486331d40d3c MyHeritage] > 
2 CONT * Name	Enoch Place
2 CONT * Gender	Male
2 CONT * Summary	Genealogy of Hannah Wilcox and her family, including marriage to Enoch Place and children.
2 CONT * Death	Jan 2 1789  Foster, Rhode Island, United States
2 CONT * Relatives	Benejah Place
2 CONT * Rufus Place
2 CONT * Article theme	Genealogy
2 CONT * Newspaper	The Boston Transcript
2 CONT * Publication	June 16 1926   Boston, Suffolk, Massachusetts, United States
2 CONT * Text	...and daughter Hannah. Widow Martha, died in Exeter, 1753. names all these sons except Edward and names daughters Hannah Place and Martha. EnochPlace died at Foster, Jan. 2. 1789; will names wife Hannah; sons Thomas, Steph
2 CONC en. Benejah, Rufus and Enoch; daughter Mary Tylor...
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
