3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizur Webster
2 CONT BIRTH	24 Aug 1767
2 CONT DEATH	8 Mar 1854 (aged 86)
2 CONT BURIAL	East Ripley Cemetery, Ripley, Chautauqua County, New York, USA, PLOT FG 71-99 Family Monument, MEMORIAL ID 10170391
2 CONT
2 CONT Tall Monumnet 
2 CONT Hon. Elizur WEBSTER died 3/8/1851 ae 80-6-11
2 CONT Wife Elizabeth (*WARREN) d 12/17/1848 ae 77-7-2
2 CONT Elizur Jr 11/19/1809 d 2/22/1871
2 CONT Wife Frances AVERILL b 9/20/1822 died 9/21/1862
2 CONT Albert H. 4/24/1844 d 9/17/1860
2 CONT his wife 
2 CONT Anna, Dau of William Henry Harrison and wife Mary DICKSON d
2 CONT 11/17/1862 ae 11/11/0
2 CONT Hon, Elizur WEBSTER, born in CT, Aug 24 1767, In October 1803 removed from Washington Co NY to Batavia, afterwards, (1808) to the town of Warsaw NY,he was the first settler of that place; where he had in the preceding June
2 CONT taken several thousand acres of the Holland land Co land. In Feb. 1837 he removed to Ripley, on the farm owned previously by Basil BURGESS, Robert DICKSON, and Henry ABELL.
2 CONT Elizur and Elizabeth had 12 children all of whom had families
2 CONT WILL;
2 CONT p. 276, Elizar Webster of town of Ripley, Chautauqua County, New York, signed 25 Jan 1851, recorded 1 Sep 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT $2000 to children of my deceased son Aroin? Arvin? Webster. $2183 to son Warren Webster. $3134 to son Chipman Webster. $2180 to son Horace Webster. $1450 to son Lemuel Webster. $2800 to son Elizar Webster. $2221.64 to son Gideon W
2 CONC ebster. $2400 to son William Harrison Webster. $1850 to daughter Eliza Young. $2166 to daughter Clarinda Hough. $3989 to daughter Harriet J. Smallwood. $10 to daughter Lucinda Norton. Remainder of estate to be divided among my chi
2 CONC ldren or their heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Executors: Joshua R. Babcock of Westfield and Jeremiah Mann of Ripley
2 CONT . 277, court 29 May 1854. Jeremiah Mann, one of the executors named in the will of Elizar Webster late of Ripley, showed that the testator died 8 Mar 1854 and presented the will for probate. Citations for a hearing 5 Jul 1854 were
2 CONC  issued to heirs and next of kin Warren Webster and Gideon Webster of Gowanda, Cattaraugus County, New York; Chipman Webster, Elizar Webster, Harriet J. Smallwood wife of John Smallwood, and William H. Webster of Ripley; Lemuel We
2 CONC bster and Clarinda Hough wife of Orson Hough of Westfield; Eliza Young wife of Andrew M. Young and Lucinda Norton wife of Elijah Norton of Warsaw, Wyoming County, New York; Horace Webster of Erie County, Pennsylvania, Alta Elliott
2 CONC  wife of William Elliott, Eliza Br…en and Alexander Webster of Olney, Richland County, Illinois, Lucinda McCluer wife of Martin McCluer and Harriet J. Myers of northern part of Illinois; Lucinda Webster and Spencer of Indiana; all
2 CONC  of full age except Lucinda Webster and Spencer Webster, with Selden Marvin Esq appointed as their guardian for the purposes of guarding their interests during probate.
2 CONT p. 277, court 5 Jul 1854. Joshua R. Babcock and Jeremiah Mann requested that the will of Elizar Webster be admitted to probate. Lemuel Webster, one of the heirs, opposed probate, alleging that the testator was not competent to mak
2 CONC e a will and that the will was not properly executed. Proceedings were adjourned to 29 Aug 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 278, court 29 Aug 1854. Hearing on probate for the will of Elizar Webster.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Mann, one of the subscribing witnesses, testified to the validity of the will. He wrote the will at the request of deceased. Deceased thought he could give each of his children $5000, and then he deducted the amount he had a
2 CONC lready advanced each child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Silas Spencer, one of the subscribing witnesses, testified about circumstances of his signing the will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John G. Hinckly gave details of a business transaction with deceased, who was about 80, in buying a mortgage of J. B. Shepard of Ashtabula, Ohio. Deceased seemed forgetful.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Francis, clerk in the land office, testified that deceased came to the office one day asking for David E. Evans, who had not been in the land office for a great many years. On another occasion asked for John B. Patterson rath
2 CONC er than John D. Patterson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elijah Norton, son-in-law of deceased, testified about business between himself, his wife, and deceased, and that deceased was forgetful. In 1851 the children of Judge Webster lived in the following places: Gideon in Gowanda, Mrs.
2 CONC  Hough and Lemuel in Westfield in town, Mrs. Smallwood, Harrison, Warren, Chipman, and Elizur in Ripley, Horace in Pennsylvania, the heirs of Arvin in Indiana or Illinois, Mrs. Norton and Mrs. Young in Warsaw, Wyoming County. Thos
2 CONC e who lived in Ripley lived within a mile of where the judge lived at Smallwoods.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter Lucinda Norton testified that her father went among his children taking receipts for what he supposed he had advanced, but the amounts were not accurate and he was confused.
2 CONT
2 CONT Orson Hough, son-in-law of deceased, moved from Pennsylvania to Chautauqua County in spring 1850. Testified about business with deceased, who in later years was confused and failing in his capacity to do business. His property con
2 CONC sisted mainly of bonds and mortgages and farms. Some of the farms were occupied by Lemuel, Smallwood, Chipman Webster in Ripley, Warren, Elizar, and Harrison.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sextus H. Hungerford, banker in the Bank of Westfield, testified that deceased had once inquired for a mortgage that he had not left with the bank, although he was convinced that the bank had it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Watson S. Hinckly testified that deceased was confused in business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geo. W. Patterson testified that he had been acquainted with Judge Webster even before he came to the county in 1822. Deceased had been failing in his last years.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Mann was recalled and testified that deceased was very clear and explicit in making the will and that deceased had been planning his will for several years.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Samllwood of Ripley, son-in-law of deceased, said that deceased lived with him for the last 15 years. His memory was not as good the last 3-4 years; I noticed him failing mentally in 1852. All of his children in Ripley were o
2 CONC f full age and frequently visited their father in 1850-1851. I have resided in Warsaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses A. Tenant testified that his son Delos rented the farm on which Chipman Webster now lives. Deceased seemed capable of doing business.
2 CONT Azariah F. Bennett visited frequently with deceased at the end of his life and did not notice any problems.
2 CONT John C. Hungerford described business transactions with deceased, who seemed to conduct business well.
2 CONT Simeon Collins of Ripley testified that deceased was in a poor state of intellectual powers starting with an accident in summer 1852 but was fit for making a will in 1851.
2 CONT Orson Hough was recalled to testify about a mortgage transaction with deceased.
2 CONT Gideon Webster of Gowanda described the land occupied by Col. Norton, that it was good land but had been neglected.
2 CONT John Smallwood was recalled to testify about the Webster farm in Warsaw.
2 CONT Elijah Norton was recalled to testify about the farm he occupied
2 CONT p. 293, court 1 Sep 1854. Court ruled that the will of Elizar Webster was valid and would be admitted to probate. Administration was granted to Joshua R. Babcock and Jeremiah Mann. Thaddeus S. May and Charles B. Brockway of Ripley
2 CONC  were appointed to appraise the estate.
2 CONT Witnesses: David Mann and Silas Spencer, both of Westfield
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription
2 CONT Hon. Elizur WEBSTER died 3/8/1851 ae 80-6-11
2 CONT
