1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''David Bishop''' was a founding pioneer of east Ashford, Windham, Connecticut. Most of the settlers came from Roxbury, MA. and settled on what is now called Woodstock Hill in 1686. The town was originally called 
2 CONC New Roxbury.  There were two settlements, East and West, and in the middle was built the Meeting House. Woodstock was the place where the famous minister to the Indians,  Rev. John Eliot,preached the gospel and made it available i
2 CONC n the local Native American language.
2 CONT
2 CONT " Ashford was also formerly called New Scituate and was founded in 1710. This area, part of the Wabbaquasset Indian country, was deeded to Captain James Fitch of Norwich in 1684 by Owaneco, sachem of the Mohegans.   Rev. James Hal
2 CONC e was another early settler. In October, 1714 further town privileges were granted and liberty to settle a minister and erect a meetinghouse. William Ward and John Perry were chosen as selectmen at the first town meeting, held ear
2 CONC ly in 1715. This green was the center of town before the separation of Eastford  (East Ashford) in 1847. The First Church of Ashford, Congregational, organized November 26, 1718 with James Hale as minister, stood on this common.
2 CONT David's death date is inferred (but not proven) by his wife's remarriage to Benjamin Allen."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''David Bishop''' (1677–1717) and his children are primarily found in Woodstock and Ashford town records. Quaker-like persecution fits the family's history, their specific footprints in Windham County records reflect the transiti
2 CONC on from the "Radical" origins of Salem to the "Separatist" movements of 18th-century Connecticut. 
2 CONT '''Quaker vs. Separatist in Ashford'''
2 CONT In the early 1700s, the line between Quakers and Separatists (New Lights) was often blurred by the authorities. Both groups rejected the established "Old Light" Congregational church and refused to pay taxes to support state-manda
2 CONC ted ministers. 
2 CONT '''The Meeting House: In 1718, just after David’s death, Ashford records show the town was struggling to build its first meeting house. David himself was on the side of dissent; on October 3, 1716, he is recorded as voting against
2 CONC  a motion related to town/church organization, a common act for those with Quaker or Separatist leanings.'''
2 CONT A "Safe" Frontier: Many families with Quaker backgrounds migrated to Ashford and neighboring Pomfret because these were "frontier" towns where the established church was not yet strong enough to enforce strict religious taxes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Evidence of Quaker Affiliation'''
2 CONT The Smithfield Connection: The Smithfield Monthly Meeting in Rhode Island (established as the Providence Monthly Meeting in 1705) was the primary spiritual hub for "Friends" in the region where the Bishop family moved.
2 CONT Uxbridge Monthly Meeting: In 1783, the Uxbridge Monthly Meeting was formally carved out of Smithfield to serve the growing Quaker community on the border of Massachusetts and Connecticut.
2 CONT Family Records: Vital records from the Uxbridge and Smithfield meetings contain multiple entries for the Bishop surname during the 1700s, including a David Bishop born in 1766 to Joseph and Elisabeth Bishop—likely a grandson or gr
2 CONC eat-grandson of the David Bishop from Ashford.
2 CONT Nature of Persecution and "Disowning"
2 CONT The Quaker records (Meeting Minutes) are known for documenting "sufferings"—the penalties Quakers endured for following their faith—and "disownings". For the Bishops, this often meant: 
2 CONT '''Marrying "Out of Meeting":''' Many Bishops in this era were "disowned" (removed from membership) because they married individuals from the Congregational or Baptist churches without following the strict Quaker process.
2 CONT Military Refusal: During the Revolutionary period, some family members were cited for refusing to participate in military drills, a hallmark Quaker stance that led to fines and public shunning by the wider community.
2 CONT '''Relocation to Ashford:''' Moving to Ashford in 1712 allowed David Bishop to live in a "frontier" zone where he could avoid the heavy religious taxation of the established coastal churches. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "sufferings" of David Bishop’s children were less about physical persecution (which their grandparents faced in Salem) and more about the economic and social hardship of being religious dissenters on the Connecticut frontier.
2 CONT While David died relatively young in 1717, his children—including John, David Jr., and Ebenezer—navigated a period where refusing to conform to the "Standing Order" (the state-mandated Congregational Church) carried heavy penaltie
2 CONC s.
2 CONT '''Disenfranchisement and Civil Status'''
2 CONT The Bishop children lived in a "limbo" status. Because they did not hold full membership in the established church, they were often:
2 CONT Barred from voting on certain town matters during the early 1720s.
2 CONT '''Denied cemetery space''': Many dissenters were forced to bury their dead on their own farms (family plots) because they were not welcome in the "consecrated" town burying grounds. This is likely why many early Bishop graves in 
2 CONC the Ashford area are unmarked or lost.
2 CONT '''Family Instability
2 CONT A significant "suffering" was the legal battle over guardianship. After David died, the court struggled to manage his estate because of his "unorthodox" family ties.'''
2 CONT His children were effectively split up. His brother Samuel Bishop of Attleborough had to intervene legally in 1725 to ensure the children received their inheritance from their grandmother, Sarah Wildes Bishop. Without this interve
2 CONC ntion, their "dissenter" status might have allowed the town to seize their assets for their upkeep as orphans.
2 CONT
2 CONT '' The Shift to "New Light" Separatism'''
2 CONT By the time the Great Awakening hit in the 1740s, David’s descendants largely moved from being "suffering Quakers" to "New Light Separatists."
2 CONT They became part of the Strict Congregationalists, who suffered even more acutely. In 1742, Connecticut passed the Anti-Itinerancy Act, making it illegal for their preferred "New Light" ministers to preach. The Bishops would have 
2 CONC faced fines just for attending these unauthorized meetings.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Seizing of Property (Distraint for Religious Taxes)
2 CONT '''In the 1700s, Ashford operated under the "Standing Order," which required all residents to pay taxes to the established Congregational Church. For families like the Bishops who held Quaker-like or Separatist views, this led to 
2 CONC specific hardships:
2 CONT The Ashford "Rate" Conflict: Town records show that in 1716, David Bishop voted against town-church measures. When dissenters refused to pay the "Minister's Rate" (salary tax) or meeting-house building fees, the town constable was
2 CONC  legally authorized to seize their physical goods.
2 CONT Method of Seizure: Property was not just land, but often "movables." Constables would seize livestock (cows/oxen), grain, or household furniture. These items were then auctioned at the "town post" to cover the unpaid religious tax
2 CONC es.
2 CONT Economic Impact: Because seized goods were often sold for far less than their value, this process frequently crippled the farming operations of dissenting families like the Bishops.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Part 2: Transition to Revolutionary Military Support'''
2 CONT By 1775, the Bishop family's stance had shifted from passive religious dissent to active political and military support for the American cause.
2 CONT The "Lexington Alarm": Ashford responded immediately to the 1775 outbreak of war. Captain Thomas Knowlton led seventy-eight men from Ashford to Massachusetts.
2 CONT Military Service of Descendants: Several Bishop descendants from the Ashford/Windham area appear in the Connecticut Third Regiment and later in Knowlton's Rangers.
2 CONT '''Family Names in Service:'''
2 CONT Samuel Bishop: A contemporary of Andrew Tracy Jr. in the nearby Newent/Lisbon area, Samuel and his sons (such as Nathaniel Bishop) were active in military governance and local defense.
2 CONT Daniel Bishop: Records for the Ashford area list Daniel Allen Jr. as an Ensign alongside the town's prominent military leaders.
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=93606
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bristol County Probate Court References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 11, 1711, Edward Bishop, Jr. made a will, which was probated May 28 of that year.
2 CONT It was witnessed by Deacon Samuel Newman, Moses Read, and Daniel Carpenter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward named the following individuals in his will:
2 CONT Sarah Bishop: Wife. Executor.
2 CONT Edward Bishop: Eldest son.
2 CONT Samuel Bishop: Son.
2 CONT William Bishop: Son.
2 CONT Jonathan Bishop: Son.
2 CONT Joseph Bishop: Son.
2 CONT '''David Bishop: Son.'''
2 CONT Benjamin Bishop: Son, adding, "if he lives to come home."
2 CONT John Bishop: Son.
2 CONT Ebenezer Bishop: Son.
2 CONT Priscilla Day: Daughter.
2 CONT Sarah Jorden: Daughter.
2 CONT Samuel Day: Son-in-law.
2 CONT Edward Day: Grandson, under age 21.
2 CONT John Day: Grandson, under age 21.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Bishop died in 1725. Bristol County Probate Records Vol. 5, p. 157, records that the Court appointed Samuel Bishop of Attleboro the administrator of his late mother's estate.
2 CONT She died intestate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 4 February 1701
2 CONT
2 CONT Childrens births confirm mother Rebeckah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Woodstock, Windham, Connecticut, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Citing Norafleming1:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Vital Records of Connecticut, Series I, Towns III, by Lucius Barnes Barbour, a compiled record of vital births, marriages and deaths for the town of Woodstock,  Connecticut.  See page 2: "A return of Mr Josiah Dwight of  Such Per
2 CONC sons as were married by him in Woodstock", then included is the marriage of David Bishop and Rebeckah Hubbard, both of Woodstock, on 4 February 1701/02.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Proprietors Records of the town of Ashford, Windham, Connecticut, 1705-1770, page 48, dated 29 December in the year of 1718, is found the following: "Voted at sd meeting that the town doe grant to the heirs of David Bisho
2 CONC p an equal share of all divisions of land". (FHC Film number 3676). From these same records, on a 1716 list of proprietors voting in Ashford was David Bishop. He does not appear on any later lists, only mention of his heirs. Also,
2 CONC  in December of 1717 is a marriage recorded of a Rebeckah Bishop to Benjamin Allen. This needs to be verified but could be David's widow. There are further records in the deeds of Ashford which many years later mention David's chi
2 CONC ldren and heirs.: put into standard format TRittel 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT David Bishop died sometime before or near the time of his mother Sarah Bishop's passing. Bristol County, Massachusetts Probate Court records (Vol. 5, p 199) indicate that on December 21, 1725, Samuel Bishop of Attleboro was appoin
2 CONC ted guardian of the children of David Bishop, late of Ashford, Hartford County, Connecticut, namely: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth Calculation:
2 CONT
2 CONT David had to have been 16 years of age by 1695. He was a signer on a petition in Salem, Massachusetts that year, under the heading "Young Men 16 years old". 1677 is an estimate based on his birth order (listed in his father's will
2 CONC  in 1711) and approximate birth dates of his siblings. As for David's place of birth, the family of Edward Bishop and Sarah Wildes Bishop was living in either Beverly or Salem Village, in Essex County, Massachusetts at the time. D
2 CONC avid didn't move from Essex County until about 1700 or 1701. His name appears on the tax lists for Essex County until those years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Settlement at Ashford:
2 CONT
2 CONT The general court, whose committee had done nothing toward laying out a town here, now reappointed a committee with more practical instructions to proceed at once with the project of establishing a town here. The committee now too
2 CONC k possession of .the township and undertook to lay it out in the name of the colony. The name Ashford was suggested by the great number of ash trees which grew in the primitive forests. The region was rough, rocky and unattractive
2 CONC , a great portion of it being covered with dense forests which abounded in wolves, bears and various species of game. This was a favorite hunting ground of the remaining Wabbaquassets, who secured large quantities of furs here, wh
2 CONC ich they furnished in trade to Mr. Corbin, who derived therefrom a considerable revenue. Only two families of white inhabitants, and they living five miles apart, were now upon the tract. The impending contest between the individu
2 CONC al proprietors already mentioned and the government of Connecticut was a serious obstacle in the way of settlement. Both parties appealed to the general court; the representatives of the Fitch title for confirmation of their title
2 CONC  and liberty to settle, and the committee to show their inability to carry out their instructions under existing circumstances. While the court was undecided as to what course to take, the claimants under Fitch pushed forward the 
2 CONC work of settlement. Philip Eastman, of Woodstock, and John Pitts, Benjamin Allen, Benjamin Russel and William Ward, of Marlborough, bought farms of James Corbin and settled on them, north of the Stoddard tract, on Still river, in 
2 CONC the summer of 1711. Houses were built, lands broken up, and a highway was laid out by these settlers. In the following year William Price, senior and junior, '''David Bishop''', Nathaniel Walker, John Chubb and John Ross bought la
2 CONC nd of Corbin and joined the eastern settlement. Daniel James and Nathaniel Fuller, of Windham, Josiah Bugbee, of Woodstock, and Samuel Rice and Philip Squier, of Concord, purchased farms of Captain Chandler in New Scituate. The co
2 CONC urt’s committee also sold some land. Homesteads were purchased of them by Isaac Kendall, William Chapman, Isaac Farrar and Simon Burton.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://connecticutgenealogy.com/windham/ashford.htm
