1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/12931412/person/26045635288/facts Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 23 2018, 21:05:03 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/112157845/esther-smith
2 CONT
2 CONT Esther Webster, dau. of Jonathan Webster, Jr. and Esther Judd, b. of record Feb. 23 (bp. Feb. 29), 1707/8 at Hartford, Conn. and bapt. at the Hartford 2nd Church. She d. Mar. 9, 1793 at Hanover, NH (family cenotaph monument, æ. 85
2 CONC .)
2 CONT
2 CONT Esther m. on an unrecorded date, but by early 1727 possibly at East Hartford, Conn., Timothy Smith, s. of Samuel Smith and his 2nd wife Mary Smith (dau. of Joseph of Hadley), b. June 1, 1702 (Hadley Genealogies) or June 10, 1702 (
2 CONC Webster Gen.) at Hadley, Mass. They had ten known children, all b. in Connecticut before relocating to Hanover, NH. For a summary of the children, see their father's memorial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edited 12/14/2019
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription
2 CONT SMITH FAMILY MONUMENT
2 CONT Esther Webster, [d.] Mar. 9, 1793 æ. 85
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to the early 1800s gravestones stated a person's age at death using a variation of the Latin Ætatis suæ (i.e., Æ, "Aged," in the XX year of one's Age), which is one year more than one's chronological age. At death Esther was
2 CONC  Æ 86, but this family monument, obviously errected and inscribed well after the early 1800s, instead uses the symbol æ. to denote each person's chronological age at death.
