2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: (1) [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000003940072503?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000082878901879 '''Marcy Robinson'''] on January 5, 1738 in Westerly Rhode Island in Westerly, Washington, R. I -
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: (2) [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000003940072503?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000090148474099 '''Esther Sheffield'''] in 1742  in Westerly, Washington, Rhode Island, USA. Esther Sheffield was born around
2 CONC  1712 to 1720 in Rhode Island, the daughter of Joseph Sheffield Jr. and Mary Earle. Edward Wilcox married an Esther Sheffield [Query-relevant Context] and Benjamin Wilcox married a different Esther Sheffield on December 16, 1746 [
2 CONC Query-relevant Context], amateur genealogists frequently merge all the children into one single, massive household.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children with Marcy Mary Robinson=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Prudence Wilcox''' (c. 1739)
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000014408161950?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000082872678872&position=11 '''Hazard Wilcox'''] - Birth circa 1740 in  Westerly, Washington, Rhode Island, United, Death: Feb 08, 1780 in
2 CONC  White Plains, Westchester, New York, United States States 
2 CONT * '''Stephen Wilcox'''  born 1743 in Richmond, Washington, Rhode Island & died SEP 06,1834 in Smyrna, Chenango, New York, USA, married Sarah Bates
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children with Esther Sheffield =
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sheffield Wilcox''' (1746–1826): He was born on April 2, 1746, in Richmond/Westerly, Rhode Island. He married Eunice Ross on December 11, 1771, in Westerly. He eventually moved out of New England and passed away in New Albany
2 CONC , Bradford County, Pennsylvania. He serves as a critical half-brother Captain Hazard Wilcox, Sr..
2 CONT * '''Pvt. Matthew Wilcox''' (1751–1833): His Revolutionary War pension application files and his physical gravestone at Jamesville Cemetery in DeWitt/Pompey, Onondaga County, New York, completely validate these precise details. Hi
2 CONC s marriage to Rachel Tillotson (1761–1818) is fully documented in both Tillotson and Wilcox family bibles.
2 CONT * '''Francis Wilcox''' (1748–1847): He was born in Rhode Island and later migrated along the same westward trajectory as his brother Matthew, eventually settling and dying in Cortland County, New York.
2 CONT * '''Edward Wilcox, Jr.''' (born 1756): He was named directly after his father. He appears alongside his father Edward Sr. in pre-war Rhode Island census fragments before the family network shifted outward to the Stonington, Conne
2 CONC cticut and New York borders.
2 CONT * '''John/Jonathan Wilcox''' (born 1757): He is recorded in the early Richmond, Rhode Island town council logs as part of Edward’s second household before the family fragmented due to the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:   Alden G. Beaman, Ph.D., Beaman, (Name: Rhode Island Genealogical Register, Oct 1979;), p. 95, "The Edward Wilcox, b. ca 1714 who mar (1) 5 Jan 1739 Mercy Robinson b. ca 1718 and (2) Esther b ca 1720 was son of Stephen(3) W
2 CONC ilcox (Stephen(2), Edward(1))." The following reference in Stonington Land Evidence (FHL #0005594 Vol 4) is the only reference we find to Edward in Stonington, CT: p471 John Goodwills of Stonington L90 paid Edward Willcocks 75A bo
2 CONC unded tree, rock, Ebenezer Sounds 8 Oct 1734, wtn. Daniel Palmer, H. Palmer, Eliphan Green The following entry in Arnold would indicate that Sheffield Wilcox, son of Edward, had moved to Richmond, RI by 1771. "WILCOX Sheffield, of
2 CONC  Edward, and Esther, of Richmond, and Eunice Ross, of Thomas, of Westerly; m. by Jeffrey Wilcox, Justice, Dec. 11, 1771. (Westerly)" Did Edward also move to Richmond? (If so, is he confused in the Richmond records with Edward (Tho
2 CONC mas, Stephen)?) A second question is where did Sheffield get his name? If we assumed that Sheffield was the son of Edward Wilcox and Amy Sheffield then the name is easy to explain. However the marriage record from Arnold indicates
2 CONC  that Sheffield's mother was Esther, not Amy. It is interesting to note that Stephen Wilcox, son of Stephen Wilcox, half-brother of Edward, was married to Mary Sheffield, sister of Amy Sheffield...who mentioned the sons of Edward 
2 CONC Wilcox (Thomas, Stephen) in her will. In addition, Stephen's brother Benjamin was married to Esther Sheffield, perhaps the sister of Amy and Mary Sheffield.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://archive.org/details/genealogicalreco00byubrow/page/32/mode/2up?q=Wilcox Edward's Mother, Martha Hazard Wilcox. by Genealogical Record of the Descendants of Thomas Brownell, 1619 to 1910]
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Hazard, daughter of Robert and Mary Brownell Hazard died 1753; married Thomas Wilcox, died 1743, and had eight children. Robert, born 1687; married Catherine Lillibridge. Stephen. Jeffery. Thomas, born October 24, 1693. Abr
2 CONC aham. George. Edward. Hannah.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 17, 17 10, he and seventeen others at Kings Town bought two thousand acres of land ordered sold by the Assembly. Her husband's will, dated April, 1728, appointed her as executrix, and her brother-in-law, Stephen Hazard, ove
2 CONC rseer. He willed "to *even sons certain land equally, viz: Eldest son Robert, Stephen, Jeffery, Thomas, Abraham, George and '''Edward ;''' to sons George and ''''Edward,''' the part of land where house stands, the north side being
2 CONC  Edward's part, and neither to sell except to the other; to wife, the stock and household goods and negro man, and also housing till youngest son''' Edward is of age, and after that she to have best room ; the young children to be
2 CONC  brought up by my wife."'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 18, 1743, she made her will which was proven January 9, 1753, calling herself, widow, Martha of Exeter. In it Thomas Sweet of North Kings Town was named as executor. She willed "to her daughter Hannah Place, interest of
2 CONC  two hundred pounds for life, and to said daughter's heirs at death ; to eldest daughter of Hannah, if she happen to have any, bed, chest, etc., and to second daughter, a brass kettle; to sons Robert, Stephen, Jeffery, Thomas, Geo
2 CONC rge.''' Edward''' and Abraham, five shillings each daughter Hannah Place, rest of estate." (Enoch Place receipted for legacy).
